    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d5">

                <pb facs="0015" n="5" sameAs="frus:frus1942China/pg5"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Madame Chiang Kai-shek
                        to Mr. Lauchlin Currie, Administrative
                        Assistant to President Roosevelt
                    </hi><anchor n="15" xml:id="d5-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-01-18">January 18,
                        1942</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Although Generalissimo repeatedly made offer through Dennys<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d5-f2"/> and later Wavell<anchor n="17" xml:id="d5-f3"/> to send two
                    Chinese army corps reinforce Burma force offer was declined on grounds that
                    Burma only needs three regiments. These regiments now sent to Keng-tung while
                    offered army corps despatched China fronts.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d5-f1" n="15" type="source" xml:id="d5-f1-content">
                <p>Copy of telegram transmitted to President Roosevelt by Mr. Currie. Photostatic
                    copy obtained from the Franklin D. Roosevelt Library, Hyde Park, N. Y.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d5-f2" n="16" xml:id="d5-f2-content">
                <p>Maj. Gen. Lancelot Ernest Dennys, British Military Attaché in China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d5-f3" n="17" xml:id="d5-f3-content">
                <p>Gen. Sir Archibald P. Wavell, British Commander in Chief, India.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
